deadlines and other issues.\nWorkshops will typically be one full day in length, although half-day and two-day proposals will also be considered. The AAAI-25 Workshop co-chairs particularly encourage the submission of proposals for interdisciplinary workshops, which may involve co-organizers that are from disciplines other than AI or may attract participants from disciplines other than AI.\nAAAI reserves the right to drop any workshop if the organizers miss the above deadlines or if there is insufficient participation. Workshops are not to be used as a vehicle for marketing products. A reduced workshop registration fee will be offered to AAAI-25 technical program registrants. Please submit your workshop proposals to the submission site no later than Monday, September 2, 2024 (no postal submissions).\nA brief description of the proposed workshop format, regarding the mix of elements such as paper presentations, invited talks, panels, and general discussion.\n\n---\n\n## Review Process - AAAI\nURL: https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-27/review-process/\n\nThe abstract deadline for AAAI submissions will be July 21, 2026,the deadline for full paper submissions will be July 28, 2026.\nThere are two cases where the existence of non-anonymous online material will not be considered a violation of AAAI-27’s blind review policy: it is acceptable for submitted work (1) to appear in a preliminary version as an unrefereed preprint (e.g., on arXiv.org, social media, personal websites) or in any workshop that does not have archival proceedings; or (2) to be discussed in research talks, even if abstracts or videos of such talks are made available online. In such cases, the AAAI-27 submission should not include citations or other pointers to the non-anonymous material and the non-anonymous online material should not reference the fact that the work was submitted to AAAI-27; violations may lead to summary rejection.\nEnsure that no information is included that would identify the authors or their affiliations; papers including such information may be summarily rejected at the discretion of the program chairs. Submissions should include references to all published literature relevant to the paper.\nThe abstract deadline for AAAI submissions will be July 21, 2026,the deadline for full paper submissions will be July 28, 2026.\nA single author feedback phase will give authors of papers from Phase 2 an opportunity to address questions or factual mistakes in reviews (from both phases), running from October 19-25, 2026.\n\n---\n\n## IUI 2027: Dates, Venue &amp; Submission Guide | zPlatform.ai\nURL: https://zplatform.ai/ai-event/iui-2027/\n\n# IUI 2027: Dates, Venue, Deadlines & Attendee Guide  \n**February 8-11, 2027**  \nTL;DR: IUI 2027, the 32nd ACM Conference on Intelligent User Interfaces, runs February 8-11, 2027 in Helsinki, Finland. The full organizing and program committee is already public, submission deadlines for papers,...  \n**TL;DR:** IUI 2027, the 32nd ACM Conference on Intelligent User Interfaces, runs **February 8-11, 2027** in **Helsinki, Finland**. The full organizing and program committee is already public, submission deadlines for papers, workshops, and tutorials run through November 2026, and the conference sits at the working intersection of human-computer interaction (HCI) and applied AI. Keynote speakers, the exact venue name, and registration pricing are not published yet. This guide covers exactly what is confirmed, what is still TBA, and who genuinely gets value out of attending.  \nI track AI and HCI conferences the same way I track AI tool deals: by checking the primary source directly instead of trusting an aggregator’s copy-paste calendar. Most “conference guide” posts for academic events just repeat whatever an aggregator scraped months ago, without ever opening the actual conference site.\n## Themes, Topics, and Submission Deadlines  \nIUI 2027’s [official call for papers](https://iui.acm.org/2027/call-for-papers/) organizes submissions into seven overlapping themes: computational innovation (interactive machine learning, recommendation systems, generative models), innovative user interfaces (AR/VR, affective and wearable interfaces), human-centered AI methods (explainable AI, trust, personalization), intelligent multimodal systems (embodied agents, AI assistants), intelligent applications (education, healthcare, automotive, smart homes), and a theme dedicated specifically to large language models and agentic AI, covering user interactions with LLMs, workplace applications, bias, and user control. That last theme is worth watching if you build AI-driven products: IUI is treating LLM and agentic-AI interaction design as its own formal category, not a subsection of “innovative interfaces.”  \nSubmission deadlines confirmed on the official site:  \nAll deadlines run to end of day Anywhere on Earth. Papers use the ACM TAPS single-column review format, target around 8,000 words excluding references, and go through double-blind review, so submissions need author names, affiliations, and self-identifying citations scrubbed first. Since today is July 15, 2026, the workshop proposal window has already closed, but the full paper, tutorial (August 20), and posters and demos (November 10) deadlines are still open.\n### When is the paper submission deadline for IUI 2027?  \nPaper abstracts are due August 13, 2026, and full papers August 20, 2026. The workshop proposal deadline (July 6, 2026) has already passed. Posters and demos are due November 10, 2026.\n\n---\n\n## AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence - AAAI\nURL: https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/\n\nThe Forty-first Conference on Artificial Intelligence will be held in Montréal, Québec, Canada from February 16-23, 2027.\nThe Forty-first Conference on Artificial Intelligence will be held in Montréal, Québec, Canada from February 16-23, 2027.\n\n---\n\n## Paper Publication and Conference Attendance - AAAI\nURL: https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-27/paper-publication-and-conference-attendance/\n\nFebruary 16 – February 23, 2027 | Montréal, Canada · AAAI-27 · EAAI-27 · IAAI-27 · Attend · Student Scholar Volunteer Program · Calls · Main Technical Track · Doctoral Consortium · EAAI-27 · Journal Track · IAAI-27 · New Faculty ...\nFebruary 16 – February 23, 2027 | Montréal, Canada · AAAI-27 · EAAI-27 · IAAI-27 · Attend · Student Scholar Volunteer Program · Calls · Main Technical Track · Doctoral Consortium · EAAI-27 · Journal Track · IAAI-27 · New Faculty Highlights · Senior Member Presentation Track · Special Track on AI for Social Impact · AAAI-27 Author Kit · Note: all deadlines are “anywhere on earth” (UTC-12) June 17, 2026 OpenReview submission site opens for author registration ·\nNote: Deadlines are track-specific and may differ from those listed above.\nTrack-specific deadlines are listed on their respective CFP.\nNote: Deadlines and instructions are track-specific and may differ from those listed below.\n\n---\n\n## AAAI-27 Submission Instructions - AAAI\nURL: https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-27/submission-instructions/\n\nEach individual author is limited ... changes). MODIFICATIONS TO SUBMISSIONS ARE ONLY ALLOWED UNTIL THE FULL PAPER DEADLINE (July 28 for Phase 1 submissions)....\nEach individual author is limited to no more than a combined limit of 10 submissions to the AAAI-27 technical track (excluding AI Alignment and AI for Social Impact tracks) and authors may not be added to papers following submission (see below for policies about author changes). MODIFICATIONS TO SUBMISSIONS ARE ONLY ALLOWED UNTIL THE FULL PAPER DEADLINE (July 28 for Phase 1 submissions).\nNote: Deadlines are track-specific and may differ from those listed above. Track-specific deadlines are listed on their respective CFP. Authors must register at the AAAI-27 web-based technical track paper submission site before they can submit abstracts and papers.\nOnce the deadline has passed, we regret that we will be unable to make individual exceptions or to correspond with authors regarding circumstances that prevented timely submission. At the time of abstract submission, authors should include a full title for their paper and a complete abstract in the fields provided.\nAuthors of such vacuous submissions will be notified that their abstract has been deleted and will not be allowed to submit a full paper. The Program Chairs also reserve the right to reject papers without review if they change their abstracts substantially between the abstract and final deadlines.\n\n---\n\n## AAAI-26 Workshop Program - AAAI\nURL: https://aaai.org/conference/aaai/aaai-26/workshops-program/\n\nSubmissions are due to the organizers on November 5, 2025 (please check individual workshop websites for extensions). Please submit your papers for review directly to the individual workshop according to their directions.\nSubmission requirements vary for each workshop. Submissions are due to the organizers on November 5, 2025 (please check individual workshop websites for extensions). Please submit your papers for review directly to the individual workshop according to their directions.\nPM Half Day Workshop – W5: AI to Accelerate Science and Engineering (AI2ASE) | Room: Conference J · W6: Addressing Challenges and Opportunities in Human-Centric Manufacturing | Room: Opal 104 · W7: Advancing Artificial Intelligence through Theory of Mind (ToM4AI) | Room: Garnet 213 · W8: Agentic AI Benchmarks and Applications for Enterprise Tasks | Room: Garnet 216\nAM Half Day Workshop – W31: AI for Environmental Science | Room: Garnet 216 · PM Half Day Workshop – W32: Artificial Intelligence with Biased or Scarce Data | Room: Garnet 216 · AM Half Day Workshop – W33: Linguistic and Cognitive Approaches to Dialogue Agents (LaCATODA’26) | Room: Peridot 206\nW36: LLM-based Multi-Agent Systems: Towards Responsible, Reliable, and Scalable Agentic Systems (LaMAS 2026) | Room: Topaz 220 – 225 · W37: Machine Ethics: from formal methods to emergent machine ethics | Room: Tourmaline 207 – 209 · W38: Machine Learning for Wireless Communication and Networks (ML4Wireless) | Room: Opal 105 -106 · W39: Neuro for AI & AI for Neuro: Towards Multi-Modal Natural Intelligence | Room: Garnet 217\n\n---\n\n","[Tool 38ffb81a linkup_web_search]: Found 10 result(s):\n\n## CVPR 2026 Workshop\nURL: https://cvpr26-advml.github.io/\n\nSubmission Site: https://openreview.net/group?id=thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/Advml Submission Due (both Paper and Supplementary Material): March 7, 2026, 11:59 PM (UTC±0) With the rapid development of multimodal foundation models and vision-language agents (VLAs), their safety and security ...\nThe 6th Workshop on Adversarial Machine Learning in Computer Vision (6th AdvML@CV): Safety of Vision-Language Agents aims to bring together researchers and practitioners from computer vision, multimodal learning, and AI safety communities to advance the frontier of robust and trustworthy vision-language agents. Continuing the success of the previous five CVPR AdvML@CV workshops, which have attracted thousands of submissions, participants, and widespread attention, the 2026 edition will feature keynote talks by leading experts, contributed papers, and an international challenge on adversarial robustness for multimodal agents.\nAlignment of vision-language agents Format: Submissions papers (.pdf format) must use the CVPR 2026 Author Kit for LaTeX/Word Zip file and be anonymized and follow CVPR 2026 author instructions. The workshop considers two types of submissions: (1) Long Paper: Papers are limited to 8 pages excluding references; (2) Extended Abstract: Papers are limited to 4 pages including references.\nThrough this workshop, we aim to foster cross-disciplinary collaboration, inspire new research directions, and catalyze the development of secure, reliable, and ethically aligned vision-language agents that can safely operate in dynamic and human-centered environments. New: Poster Presentation Location: Board #248 - #255 in Exhibit Hall A, 15:00 - 18:00. New: The Phase 2 dataset of Challenge is now available. New: Phase 2 of the challenge has begun. Important: The submission deadline has been extended to Mar. 7, 2026 (23:59, UTC±0).\nSubmission Site: https://openreview.net/group?id=thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/Advml Submission Due (both Paper and Supplementary Material): March 7, 2026, 11:59 PM (UTC±0) With the rapid development of multimodal foundation models and vision-language agents (VLAs), their safety and security risks have become important concerns for both academia and industry.\n\n---\n\n## CVPR 2026 Workshop征稿｜第六届AdvML@CV：多模态大模型智能体安全\nURL: https://sechub.in/view/3173898\n\n[机器之心](/feed/1262)2026-02-09 14:55:21\n# CVPR 2026 Workshop征稿｜第六届AdvML@CV：多模态大模型智能体安全  \n[机器之心](/feed/1262)2026-02-09 14:55:21  \nIEEE/CVF 计算机视觉与模式识别会议 CVPR 2026 将于 2026 年 6 月 3 日至 6 月 7 日在美国科罗拉多州丹佛举办。我们将在 CVPR 期间举办第六届对抗机器学习计算机视觉研讨会（6th AdvML@CV），Workshop 预计安排在 6 月 3 日或 6 月 4 日。  \n本届主题聚焦：**Safety of Vision-Language Agents（视觉-语言智能体安全）**。  \n**主题聚焦：视觉-语言智能体的安全与鲁棒性**  \n多模态基础模型推动了视觉理解、生成与推理能力的跃迁，也让 Vision-Language Agents（视觉-语言智能体）迅速成为「感知——语言推理——行动规划」一体化的新范，在无人驾驶、智能机器人等领域具有广阔应用前景。  \n但随着智能体自主性增强，攻击面也从传统像素级扰动扩展到更复杂的安全风险：例如对抗提示（Adversarial Prompts）、指令注入（Instruction Injection）、Jailbreak 操控等，它们可能扰乱推理链条、误导感知决策，甚至诱发危险行为。  \n我们希望通过本次 Workshop，汇聚计算机视觉、多模态学习与 AI Safety 社区的研究者与工程实践者，共同推进安全、鲁棒、可信的视觉-语言智能体研究与落地。  \n**论文征稿**  \n**本次研讨会诚邀与以下主题相关（但不限于）的投稿：**  \n- Attack and defense on vision-language agents\n- Datasets and benchmarks that could evaluate vision-language agents\n- Adversarial / Jailbreak attacks on vision-language agents\n- Improving the robustness of agents or deep learning systems\n- Interpreting and understanding model robustness, especially agentic AI\n- Adversarial attacks for social good\n- Alignment of vision-language agents  \n**投稿类型与格式要求：**  \n- Long Paper：正文最多 8 页（不含参考文献）\n- Extended Abstract：正文最多 4 页（含参考文献）\n- 论文需匿名，并使用 CVPR 2026 Author Kit 模板撰写（LaTeX/Word 均可）、\n- 被录用论文可选择收录至 CVF & IEEE Xplore Proceedings  \n**重要日期**  \n- Abstract Submission Deadline: 2026/03/05\n- Paper Submission Deadline: 2026/03/05\n- Author Notification: 2026/03/17\n- Camera-Ready Deadline: 2026/04/01\n- CVPR 2026 Conference: 2026/06/03  \n**演讲嘉宾**  \n**组织团队**\n#### **Program Committee**  \n**Workshop Sponsor**  \n**投稿入口与会议信息**  \n欢迎转发给有相关研究方向的同学与合作伙伴，我们期待在研讨会现场与大家交流！  \n- Workshop 官网：https://cvpr26-advml.github.io/\n- OpenReview 投稿入口  \n**：**https://openreview.net/group?id=thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/Advml\n\n---\n\n## Workshop on Computer Vision in the Wild 2026\nURL: https://computer-vision-in-the-wild.github.io/cvpr-2026/\n\nWorkshop Paper Submission Portal: [Open Review] Submission Deadline: April 15th, 2026 Acceptance Notification: May 10th, 2026 Camera-ready Submission May 30th, 2026 For more information about the paper submission, please reach out the workshop ...\nTher review process is double-blind, and the accepted papers are NOT archived in CVPR 2025 Proceeding. Workshop Paper Submission Portal: [Open Review] Submission Deadline: April 15th, 2026 Acceptance Notification: May 10th, 2026 Camera-ready Submission May 30th, 2026 For more information about the paper submission, please reach out the workshop organizers. We introduce two new challenges to evaluate the performance of large vision models in the wild:\nThe 5th Workshop on Computer Vision in the Wild Theme: Building Multimodal AI Agents with Verbal, Spatial and Temporal Intelligence Date: June 3-4 | Location: Denver Convention Center, Denver CO · As artificial intelligence continues to evolve, the intersection of vision, language, and action is becoming central to systems that must operate reliably in unconstrained, real world settings. The 5th Workshop on Computer Vision in the Wild (CVinW) at CVPR 2026 aims to bring together researchers and practitioners advancing multimodal AI agents that can perceive, reason, and act across digital and physical environments, while highlighting the capabilities where today’s models still fall short.\nNew Metrics and Benchmarks: Novel ways to evaluate existing LMMs and large vision models for task-level transfer and open-set visual recognition. We accept abstract submissions to our workshop. All submissions shall have maximally 8 pages (excluding references) following the CVPR 2025 author guidelines.\nBuilding on the success of our previous workshops: CVPR 2025 CVinW Workshop, CVPR 2024 CVinW Workshop, CVPR 2023 CVinW Workshop and ECCV 2022 CVinW Workshop, this year’s edition focuses on the intersection of large multimodal models (LMMs) and vision-language-action (VLA) systems, with particular emphasis on fine grained spatiotemporal reasoning, causal inference, long horizon planning and memory, and robust tool use. The workshop focuses on moving beyond static understanding toward agents that perceive, reason, and act in dynamic environments, including interactive digital settings and embodied physical interaction.\n\n---\n\n## Call for Papers | GRAIL-V @ CVPR 2026\nURL: https://grailworkshops.github.io/cfp/\n\nAt GRAIL-V we bring together CV, IR, NLP, and HCI communities to advance unified methods and evaluation practices for visual–text search, multimodal tool use, and calibrated decision-making. This is the CVPR 2026 workshop CFP for multimodal generative/understanding models and agents that plan, retrieve, reason and verify with an evidence-centric systems. We invite papers and demos on grounded vision-language systems that plan, retrieve, reason, and verify with evidence. We welcome submissions that bridge multimodal perception and actionable decision-making.\nAt GRAIL-V we bring together CV, IR, NLP, and HCI communities to advance unified methods and evaluation practices for visual–text search, multimodal tool use, and calibrated decision-making. This is the CVPR 2026 workshop CFP for multimodal generative/understanding models and agents that plan, retrieve, reason and verify with an evidence-centric systems. We invite papers and demos on grounded vision-language systems that plan, retrieve, reason, and verify with evidence. We welcome submissions that bridge multimodal perception and actionable decision-making.\nPresent at the workshop Camera-ready after reviews; at least one author presents in person per CVPR policy. Technical merit, grounded evaluation, efficiency, reproducibility, and broader safety considerations. Accepted papers appear as posters or short orals. At least one author presents in person. Same-employer, advisor-advisee, recent co-author, or close personal conflicts. ... Extended deadline. OpenReview submission closes at 23:59 AoE.\nWhat should I include to make my submission strong? Clear evidence grounding (citations or region/page/moment provenance), realistic efficiency reporting (latency/memory/cost), and reproducible artifacts with licenses when possible. ... Yes. Accepted papers appear in the CVPR 2026 Workshop Proceedings (archival).\nArchival short paper Early or in-progress ideas that will appear in the CVPR workshop proceedings. Demo / system paper Working systems with grounded evidence, evaluation, and reproducible artifacts. Benchmark / dataset New tasks, datasets, or evaluation harnesses for grounded retrieval and verification. Follow CVPR rules Use the CVPR 2026 author guidelines (template, anonymization, ethics).\n\n---\n\n## [CFP] GRAIL-V Workshop @CVPR 26 on Multimodal Retrieval and Agentic-Vision | ACL Member Portal\nURL: https://www.aclweb.org/portal/content/cfp-grail-v-workshop-cvpr-26-multimodal-retrieval-and-agentic-vision\n\nFebruary 02, 2026 | BY hlp276  \nEvent Notification Type:  \nCall for Papers  \nAbbreviated Title:  \nLocation:  \nWednesday, 3 June 2026  \nState:  \nCountry:  \nContact Email:  \nCity:  \nContact:  \nHitesh Laxmichand Patel  \nWebsite:  \nSubmission Deadline:  \nThursday, 5 March 2026  \n**GRAIL-V (Denver, June 3–4)** at CVPR 20266 bridges the CV, IR, NLP, and HCI communities to advance robust, deployable agentic systems. As AI agents increasingly plan, retrieve, reason and act across diverse data sources, we aim to solve critical challenges across multimodal tool use, visual-text search, and calibrated decision-making.\n> **Keynote Speakers:**[(UT Austin)]()**Kristen Grauman**[(UNC Chapel Hill)]()**Mohit Bansal**[(UPenn)]()**Dan Roth**[(Meta)]()**Scott Wen-tau YihIndustry Panel:**Hosted by**Oracle AI**on Multimodal Agentic Systems in Production.**Research Themes:Multimodal Retrieval:**Scaling search across images, video, charts, and UI.**Agentic Planning:**Tool routing, memory, and safe multi-step workflows.**Generative Handling:**Integrating generation/editing tools into agents.**Benchmarks & Evaluation:**New methodologies for assessing agentic reasoning and reliability.**Grounding & Evidence:**Citation provenance and audit-ready faithfulness.**Logistics:Paper Submission Deadline:**March 5, 2026**Notification:**March 18, 2026**Camera Ready:**April 5, 2026**Website:**[https://grailworkshops.github.io/]()**Submission Link:**[OpenReview]()**Organizing Committee:**[- Principal Applied Scientist, Oracle AI]()**Amit Agarwal**[- Associate Prof of Computing and Augmented Intelligence, Arizona State University]()**Vivek Gupta**[- Associate Prof, University of Utah]()**Vivek Srikumar**[- Senior Director, Oracle AI]()**Tao Sheng**[- Chair Professor, KAIST]()**Alice Oh**[- Co-Founder Adaption Labs, ex-VP Research Cohere]()**Sara Hooker**[- Principal Applied Scientist, Oracle AI]()**Jyotika Singh**[- Principal Applied Scientist, Oracle AI]()**Hitesh Laxmichand Patel**\n\n---\n\n## CVPR Workshop &#8211; Halıcıoğlu Data Science Institute &#8211; UC San Diego\nURL: https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-workshop-trustworthy-robust-uncertainty-aware-and-explainable-visual-intelligence-and-beyond/\n\n• Uncertainty estimation and trust calibration\n• Concept bottleneck & modular architectures\n• Alignment, safety, and ethical considerations in vision models\n• Human-in-the-loop evaluation and Evaluation benchmarks\n• Trustworthy deployment in high-stakes domains\n• Vision-language and multimodal reasoning under uncertainty  \nSubmission are now open through OpenReview at:  \n[https://openreview.net/group?](https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://openreview.net/group?id=thecvf.com%2ACVPR%2A2026%2AWorkshop%2ATRUE-V__%3BLy8vLw%21%21Mih3wA%21HpB41qN8XkQGSxRzPJSCqrN5Ar_zq2CgZu-WK5w5lSCVTK0enwzss4jU5sIPdecbzKiXwhCGVcF0im_6s7oGADw%24).  \nWe welcome and encourage the authors to consult the submission guidelines and important dates listed on the workshop website:[https://trustworthy-](https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://trustworthy-ai-workshop.github.io/cvpr2026-TRUE-V/?utm_source=chatgpt.com__%3B%21%21Mih3wA%21HpB41qN8XkQGSxRzPJSCqrN5Ar_zq2CgZu-WK5w5lSCVTK0enwzss4jU5sIPdecbzKiXwhCGVcF0im_6cUPC0N4%24)  \nThe paper submission deadline is Mar 20, 2026 at 23:59 AoE. Accepted papers will be presented as posters, with a subset selected for spotlight talks. Please feel free to reach out to the organizers directly if you have any questions.  \nWe welcome your submissions and look forward to your contributions! Please feel free to forward this CFP to your networks.  \nThis workshop is organized by:\nThis workshop is organized by:  \n– Lily Weng (UC San Diego,[lweng@ucsd.edu](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-workshop-trustworthy-robust-uncertainty-aware-and-explainable-visual-intelligence-and-beyond/mailto:lweng@ucsd.edu)),\n– Nghia Hoang (Washington State University, [trongnghia.hoang@](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-workshop-trustworthy-robust-uncertainty-aware-and-explainable-visual-intelligence-and-beyond/mailto:trongnghia.hoang@wsu.edu)),\n– Tammy Riklin Raviv (Ben Gurion University, [rrtammy@bgu.ac.il](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-workshop-trustworthy-robust-uncertainty-aware-and-explainable-visual-intelligence-and-beyond/mailto:rrtammy@bgu.ac.il))\n– Giuseppe Raffa (Intel Labs, [giuseppe.raffa@intel.com](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-workshop-trustworthy-robust-uncertainty-aware-and-explainable-visual-intelligence-and-beyond/mailto:giuseppe.raffa@intel.com)\n– Arno Blaas (Apple, [ablaas@apple.com](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-workshop-trustworthy-robust-uncertainty-aware-and-explainable-visual-intelligence-and-beyond/mailto:ablaas@apple.com))\n– Eunji Kim (Amazon, [kce407@snu.ac.kr](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-workshop-trustworthy-robust-uncertainty-aware-and-explainable-visual-intelligence-and-beyond/mailto:kce407@snu.ac.kr))\n– Bhavya Kailkhura (LLNL, [kailkhura1@llnl.gov](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-workshop-trustworthy-robust-uncertainty-aware-and-explainable-visual-intelligence-and-beyond/mailto:kailkhura1@llnl.gov))\n– Kowshik Thopalli (LLNL, [thopalli1@llnl.gov](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-workshop-trustworthy-robust-uncertainty-aware-and-explainable-visual-intelligence-and-beyond/mailto:thopalli1@llnl.gov))\n\n---\n\n## 2nd 3D-LLM/VLA Workshop | CVPR 2026\nURL: https://3d-llm-vla.github.io/\n\nThe paper submission deadline is April 26, 2026. The workshop will be held in-person on June 3, 2026 at CVPR 2026 in Room 1CD, Denver, CO, USA.\nThe paper submission deadline is April 26, 2026. The workshop will be held in-person on June 3, 2026 at CVPR 2026 in Room 1CD, Denver, CO, USA.\nAuthors of accepted papers retain the full copyright of their work and are free to submit extended versions to conferences or journals. June 3, 2026 | Room 1CD | CVPR 2026, Denver, CO, USA ... EthosVLA: A Constitutional Safety Framework for Vision-Language-Action Models in Physical 3D Environments · CausalScene: Learning Causal 3D Scene Graphs for Counterfactual Reasoning in Embodied Agents\nBuilding on the momentum and insights from our first workshop at CVPR 2025, this 2nd edition will continue to provide a unique platform for discussing the integration of language and 3D perception. The workshop will deepen the collaboration established in the first edition and further advance the state-of-the-art in 3D-LLM/VLA research. ... We invite submissions of papers related to the integration of language and 3D perception, with a focus on developing embodied agents and robots.\nWelcome: The 2nd edition of the 3D-LLM/VLA Workshop will be held at CVPR 2026! ... This workshop addresses a critical gap in current AI research by focusing on the integration of language and 3D perception, which is essential for developing embodied agents and robots, especially considering the recent rise of multimodal LLMs and vision-language-action (VLA) models.\n\n---\n\n## CVPR 2026 Workshop 征稿｜AdvML@CV 2026：Safety of Vision-Language Agents\nURL: https://sechub.in/view/3171811\n\n[机器之心](/feed/1262)2026-02-04 12:06:44\n# CVPR 2026 Workshop 征稿｜AdvML@CV 2026：Safety of Vision-Language Agents  \n[机器之心](/feed/1262)2026-02-04 12:06:44  \nIEEE/CVF 计算机视觉与模式识别会议 CVPR 2026 将于 2026年6月3日–6月7日 在美国科罗拉多州丹佛举办。我们将在 CVPR 期间举办 第六届对抗机器学习计算机视觉研讨会（6th AdvML@CV），Workshop 预计安排在 6月3日或6月4日。  \n本届主题聚焦：Safety of Vision-Language Agents（视觉-语言智能体安全）。  \n**主题聚焦：视觉-语言智能体的安全与鲁棒性**  \n多模态基础模型推动了视觉理解、生成与推理能力的跃迁，也让 Vision-Language Agents（视觉-语言智能体） 迅速成为“感知—语言推理—行动规划”一体化的新范式。  \n但随着智能体自主性增强，攻击面也从传统像素级扰动扩展到更复杂的安全风险：例如 对抗提示（adversarial prompts）、指令注入（instruction injection）、jailbreak 操控 等，它们可能扰乱推理链条、误导感知决策，甚至诱发危险行为。  \n我们希望通过本次 Workshop，汇聚计算机视觉、多模态学习与 AI Safety 社区的研究者与工程实践者，共同推进安全、鲁棒、可信的视觉-语言智能体研究与落地。  \n**论文征稿**  \n本次研讨会诚邀与以下主题相关（但不限于）的投稿：  \n•Attack and defense on vision-language agents  \n•Datasets and benchmarks that could evaluate vision-language agents  \n•Adversarial / Jailbreak attacks on vision-language agents  \n•Improving the robustness of agents or deep learning systems  \n•Interpreting and understanding model robustness, especially agentic AI  \n•Adversarial attacks for social good  \n•Alignment of vision-language agents  \n投稿类型与格式要求：  \n•Long Paper：正文最多8 页（不含参考文献）  \n•Extended Abstract：正文最多4 页（含参考文献）  \n•论文需匿名，并使用 CVPR 2026 Author Kit 模板撰写（LaTeX/Word 均可）  \n•被录用论文可选择收录至 CVF & IEEE Xplore Proceedings  \n**重要日期**  \n•Abstract Submission Deadline：2026/03/05  \n•Paper Submission Deadline：2026/03/05  \n•Author Notification：2026/03/17  \n•Camera-Ready Deadline：2026/04/01  \n•CVPR 2026 Conference：2026/06/03  \n**演讲嘉宾**  \n**组织团队**\n•Camera-Ready Deadline：2026/04/01  \n•CVPR 2026 Conference：2026/06/03  \n**演讲嘉宾**  \n**组织团队**  \n**Program Committee**  \n**竞赛承办协办单位**  \n**投稿入口与会议信息**  \nWorkshop 官网与投稿入口见文末链接。  \n欢迎转发给有相关研究方向的同学与合作伙伴，我们期待在研讨会现场与大家交流！  \nWorkshop 官网：\nhttps://cvpr26-advml.github.io/\nOpenReview 投稿入口：\nhttps://openreview.net/group?id=thecvf.com/CVPR/2026/Workshop/Advml\n\n---\n\n## CVPR Call for Papers Deadline &#8211; Halıcıoğlu Data Science Institute &#8211; UC San Diego\nURL: https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-call-for-papers-deadline/\n\nGive us a call or drop by anytime, we endeavor to answer all inquiries within 24 hours.  \nPO Box 16122 Collins Street West Victoria, Australia  \ninfo@domain.com / example@domain.com  \nPhone: + (066) 0760 0260 / + (057) 0760 0560  \nWe are excited to announce theCVPR2026 Workshop on[Trustworthy, Robust, Uncertainty-Aware, and Explainable Visual Intelligence and Beyond](https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://trustworthy-ai-workshop.github.io/cvpr2026-TRUE-V/__;!!Mih3wA!HpB41qN8XkQGSxRzPJSCqrN5Ar_zq2CgZu-WK5w5lSCVTK0enwzss4jU5sIPdecbzKiXwhCGVcF0im_6jEZQ6Sk$) (TRUE-V), which will be held in Denver, Colorado, USA on June 3 or 4, 2026.  \nModern vision and vision-language systems are increasingly deployed in safety-critical and real-world settings, yet they remain opaque, brittle, and difficult to calibrate. TRUE-V aims to advance principled foundations and practical methodologies for trustworthy visual intelligence, spanning interpretability, robustness, uncertainty, alignment, and responsible deployment.  \nWe welcome theoretical, methodological, and applied contributions that push forward the science and practice of trustworthy AI in vision and beyond. The topics of interest includes aspects that can help to advance Trustworthy Visual Intelligence, including (but not limited to):  \n• Interpretable and explainable computer vision\n• Robustness and reliability under distribution shift\n• Uncertainty estimation and trust calibration\n• Concept bottleneck & modular architectures\n• Uncertainty estimation and trust calibration\n• Concept bottleneck & modular architectures\n• Alignment, safety, and ethical considerations in vision models\n• Human-in-the-loop evaluation and Evaluation benchmarks\n• Trustworthy deployment in high-stakes domains\n• Vision-language and multimodal reasoning under uncertainty  \nSubmission are now open through OpenReview at:  \n[https://openreview.net/group?](https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://openreview.net/group?id=thecvf.com%2ACVPR%2A2026%2AWorkshop%2ATRUE-V__%3BLy8vLw%21%21Mih3wA%21HpB41qN8XkQGSxRzPJSCqrN5Ar_zq2CgZu-WK5w5lSCVTK0enwzss4jU5sIPdecbzKiXwhCGVcF0im_6s7oGADw%24).  \nWe welcome and encourage the authors to consult the submission guidelines and important dates listed on the workshop website:[https://trustworthy-](https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://trustworthy-ai-workshop.github.io/cvpr2026-TRUE-V/?utm_source=chatgpt.com__%3B%21%21Mih3wA%21HpB41qN8XkQGSxRzPJSCqrN5Ar_zq2CgZu-WK5w5lSCVTK0enwzss4jU5sIPdecbzKiXwhCGVcF0im_6cUPC0N4%24)  \nThe paper submission deadline is Mar 20, 2026 at 23:59 AoE. Accepted papers will be presented as posters, with a subset selected for spotlight talks. Please feel free to reach out to the organizers directly if you have any questions.  \nWe welcome your submissions and look forward to your contributions! Please feel free to forward this CFP to your networks.  \nThis workshop is organized by:\nThis workshop is organized by:  \n– Lily Weng (UC San Diego,[lweng@ucsd.edu](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-call-for-papers-deadline/mailto:lweng@ucsd.edu)),\n– Nghia Hoang (Washington State University, [trongnghia.hoang@](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-call-for-papers-deadline/mailto:trongnghia.hoang@wsu.edu)),\n– Tammy Riklin Raviv (Ben Gurion University, [rrtammy@bgu.ac.il](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-call-for-papers-deadline/mailto:rrtammy@bgu.ac.il))\n– Giuseppe Raffa (Intel Labs, [giuseppe.raffa@intel.com](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-call-for-papers-deadline/mailto:giuseppe.raffa@intel.com)\n– Arno Blaas (Apple, [ablaas@apple.com](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-call-for-papers-deadline/mailto:ablaas@apple.com))\n– Eunji Kim (Amazon, [kce407@snu.ac.kr](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-call-for-papers-deadline/mailto:kce407@snu.ac.kr))\n– Bhavya Kailkhura (LLNL, [kailkhura1@llnl.gov](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-call-for-papers-deadline/mailto:kailkhura1@llnl.gov))\n– Kowshik Thopalli (LLNL, [thopalli1@llnl.gov](https://datascience.ucsd.edu/event/cvpr-call-for-papers-deadline/mailto:thopalli1@llnl.gov))\n\n---\n\n## CVPR 2026 Call for Workshops\nURL: https://cvpr.thecvf.com/Conferences/2026/CallForWorkshops\n\nIf the workshop hosts a competition, describe it and: (a) Explain which datasets will be used, (b) Whether the datasets are already available or not; in the latter case, provide an estimate when the datasets will be available and describe your contingency plan in case of delays; (c) Ethical considerations for the datasets, (d) How submissions will be evaluated, (e) The timeline for the competition (start, submission deadline, decisions to participants). If in-person posters are included, how many are expected? Special space or equipment requests, if any. Proposals failing to comply with the guidelines will be desk-rejected. The CVPR 2026 organizers will collect workshop registrations, provide facilities, and distribute electronic copies of the workshop proceedings.\nIf the workshop hosts a competition, describe it and: (a) Explain which datasets will be used, (b) Whether the datasets are already available or not; in the latter case, provide an estimate when the datasets will be available and describe your contingency plan in case of delays; (c) Ethical considerations for the datasets, (d) How submissions will be evaluated, (e) The timeline for the competition (start, submission deadline, decisions to participants). If in-person posters are included, how many are expected? Special space or equipment requests, if any. Proposals failing to comply with the guidelines will be desk-rejected. The CVPR 2026 organizers will collect workshop registrations, provide facilities, and distribute electronic copies of the workshop proceedings.\nNot every workshop will be assigned to a track – in fact, we encourage submission of workshops on emerging topics which will not be part of any particular track. Workshops will follow the format of CVPR 2026 (in-person and hybrid). More information will be disseminated closer to the conference date. There will be no fully virtual workshops. If workshop organizers intend to peer-review and publish accepted papers in conjunction with CVPR 2026 proceedings, please note the following:\nIf including paper submissions, provide: (a) Tentative program committee, (b) Paper review timeline, (c) Will these papers be published in proceedings? Note that paper submissions must adhere to the CVPR 2026 paper submission style, format, length restrictions, and camera-ready timeline.\nNEW for CVPR 2026: if workshop organizers anticipate certain invited speaker(s) to draw large crowds of >300 people, please inform CVPR workshop program chairs by April 1, 2026. This is crucial to allow CVPR Chairs to effectively assign rooms and manage crowds. Datasets used in CVPR workshop challenges may recommend citation of the primary paper describing the dataset.\n\n---\n\n","[Tool ed3cc634 linkup_web_search]: Found 10 result(s):\n\n## AI Deadlines – AI Conference Call For Paper Deadlines\nURL: http://aideadlines.org/\n\nFebruary 16-23, 2027.\nFebruary 16-23, 2027.\nMay 16-21, 2027.\nFebruary 8-11, 2027.\n\n---\n\n## 2027 Workshop Information\nURL: https://wacv.thecvf.com/Conferences/2027/WorkshopInfo\n\n## Important Dates  \n- Workshop proposal deadline: August 1, 2026, 11:59 PM AOE\n- Workshop acceptance notification: August 10, 2026\n- Workshop dates: January 4th - 5th, 2027  \nWe invite proposals for workshops to be held in conjunction with WACV 2027, which will take place in person in Orlando, FL. Workshops are scheduled for January 4th and 5th, 2027.  \nWorkshops should focus on topics related to computer vision and its applications, as well as interdisciplinary themes, emerging challenges, or community-building initiatives. Proposals that explore novel application areas or foster collaborations between computer vision and other scientific domains are especially encouraged.  \nWorkshop organizers will have significant autonomy over the format, style, and content of their sessions. They will be responsible for: Managing the workshop's technical program; Inviting expert speakers; and Maintaining a dedicated workshop website.  \nAll workshop organizers and presenters must participate in person. Virtual workshops or remote presentations will not be accommodated.  \nAccepted workshops will be held on either January 4th or 5th, 2027.\n## Submission Guidelines  \nWorkshop proposals must follow the official template (availableHere) and must not exceed three (3) pages, including all content. Proposals exceeding the page limit may be rejected without review.Upon acceptance, organizers are required to launch a public workshop website within one week of notification.The website must include:  \n- Call for Papers (CFP)\n- Submission instructions\n- Important dates\n- Program details\n- Contact information\n- Clarity on whether accepted papers will be published in the WACV 2027 proceedings  \nWorkshop proposals should be submitted in the form of a single PDF throughOpenReview:  \nWe especially welcome workshops that aim to strengthen or establish new communities within the field of computer vision.  \nProposals will be evaluated by the workshop chairs and conference organizers, with an eye towards selecting high-quality workshops on a diverse set of topics that will inform and inspire the community.\n## For Accepted Workshops – Please Read Carefully  \nAccepted workshops will be held on January 4th or 5th, 2027.  \nAt leastone workshop organizer and all presenters must attend in person.\nVirtual workshops or fully remote presentations willnotbe accommodated (though you may independently arrange for a limited number of virtual talks if absolutely necessary).  \nOrganizers are expected to set up your workshop website and issue a call for papers (CFP, if applicable) by August 25, 2026 , and send us the link as soon as possible so it can be added to the official WACV 2027 website.  \nOn your workshop webpage, please clearly indicate whether accepted papers will be published in the WACV 2027 proceedings. Paper reviews for WACV 2027 workshops should run through OpenReview (i.e., no CMT).  \nFinally, note the following key timeline:  \n- Workshop websites live by: August 25, 2026\n- Author notification deadline (for archival papers): October 30, 2026\n- Metadata of accepted papers due to IEEE (for archival papers): November 2, 2026\n- Camera-ready deadline (for archival papers): November 20, 2026  \nThe submission deadline for camera-ready papers is November 20, 2026, at 11:59 PM Pacific Time. This deadline applies to all accepted papers. Papers that arrive after the deadline may not appear in the conference proceedings and in IEEE Xplore.\n\n---\n\n## AI Conference Deadlines 2027: NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, CVPR | OpenCurious\nURL: https://www.opencurious.com/ai-conference-deadlines\n\nIt reviews through ACL Rolling Review (ARR), so commitment deadlines follow monthly ARR cycles.Call for papers ... ICCV, the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision, is one of the top computer-vision conferences and is held in odd-numbered years, alternating with ECCV.\nLive countdowns to 2027 submission deadlines and calls for papers for the top AI, ML, CV and NLP conferences. Free, filterable by field.\nSubmission deadlines and calls for papers for ICLR 2027, ICML 2027, NeurIPS 2027, CVPR 2027, AISTATS 2027 and AAAI 2027, with live Anywhere-on-Earth (AoE) countdowns.\nLive countdowns to the next paper submission deadlines and calls for papers (CFP) for the top AI, machine learning, computer vision and NLP conferences, including ICLR 2027, ICML 2027, NeurIPS 2027, AISTATS 2027, CVPR 2027, AAAI 2027, ACL, KDD, IJCAI, ICCV and more.\nIt reviews through ACL Rolling Review (ARR), so commitment deadlines follow monthly ARR cycles.Call for papers ... ICCV, the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision, is one of the top computer-vision conferences and is held in odd-numbered years, alternating with ECCV.\n\n---\n\n## ICCV 2027 - AI Conference Deadlines\nURL: https://mlciv.com/ai-deadlines/conference/?id=iccv27\n\nCountdowns to top CV/NLP/ML/Robotics/AI conference deadlines\n\n---\n\n## AI Conference Deadlines - Jie Cao | Dialogue, NLP, ML\nURL: https://mlciv.com/ai-deadlines/\n\nNote: CHI 2027 papers (AoE): no abstract deadline. Submission site opens July 30, 2026; full paper deadline September 10, 2026 (includes videos and supplementary materials). Revise-and-resubmit phase with resubmission December 3, 2026; final notification December 17, 2026.\nOctober 2027. Hong Kong. Note: Predicted deadline based on historical ICCV biennial pattern (past deadlines — 2025 Mar 7, 2023 Mar 8). Host city Hong Kong confirmed by CVF; dates and deadline not yet announced. Please verify on the official conference website.\nNote: Mandatory abstract deadline on February 2, 2026. Workshop & Tutorial proposals also due February 9, 2026. More info here. ... October 24-29, 2026. TBA. Note: All submissions via ACL Rolling Review (ARR). More info here . More info here. ... May 3-8, 2026. Barcelona, Spain. ... May 17-21, 2027.\nNote: CHI 2027 papers (AoE): no abstract deadline. Submission site opens July 30, 2026; full paper deadline September 10, 2026 (includes videos and supplementary materials). Revise-and-resubmit phase with resubmission December 3, 2026; final notification December 17, 2026.\nNote: Predicted — COLING 2027 expected to use ARR, with commitment from the October 2026 cycle (ARR submission deadline Oct 12, 2026). Host Macau and dates May 9-14 confirmed; ARR participation not yet officially announced. Please verify on the official conference website.\n\n---\n\n## AI Agent Event - Call for Papers\nURL: https://www.aiagentevent.com/east/call-for-speakers.aspx\n\nClick here to submit your session proposal for AI Agent Event 2027. Case studies, technical sessions, workshops, and panel proposals are now being accepted for AI Agent Event 2027. The AI Agent Event Editorial Team is seeking enterprise practitioners, AI developers, technology innovators, researchers, solution providers, and business leaders with real-world experience building, deploying, governing, and scaling AI agents and Autonomous AI systems. We also welcome speaker recommendations for sessions currently under development. Final Submission Deadline: September 9  \nAI agents have moved beyond experimentation. Organizations are now deploying Autonomous AI to automate business processes, improve customer experiences, accelerate software development, strengthen cybersecurity, optimize operations, and transform virtually every department of the enterprise. AI Agent Event is dedicated to helping organizations move from proof-of-concept to production by exploring the strategies, architectures, technologies, and real-world deployments that deliver measurable business value. Attendees aren't looking for theory. They're looking for practical guidance from organizations already putting Autonomous AI to work.  \nSelected speakers will present to an audience of:  \nIf selected, you'll receive a complimentary All-Access Speaker Pass (a $1,799 value) providing admission to every conference, keynote, networking event, and exhibit hall throughout the ITEXPO #TECHSUPERSHOW.\nWhile Generative AI creates content, Autonomous AI takes action. Organizations everywhere are moving beyond AI that simply answers questions toward intelligent systems capable of planning, reasoning, making decisions, coordinating workflows, and completing complex business tasks with minimal human intervention. AI Agent Event is the premier conference dedicated to this next generation of enterprise AI. Our audience is actively evaluating technologies, platforms, frameworks, and deployment strategies that enable Autonomous AI to deliver measurable business results.  \nWe are particularly interested in educational sessions, customer case studies, technical presentations, and panel discussions covering topics such as:  \nOur attendees consistently rank customer deployments among the most valuable sessions. We encourage presentations that demonstrate measurable business outcomes, implementation lessons, deployment challenges, and best practices from organizations successfully using Autonomous AI today.  \nAI Agent Event 2027 is focused on practical education-not product pitches. If you've successfully deployed Autonomous AI, built intelligent agent platforms, or helped organizations transform business operations through AI-driven automation, we invite you to share your expertise with one of the industry's most influential audiences.  \nAI Agent Event is one of several technology conferences taking place during the ITEXPO #TECHSUPERSHOW, alongside:\nTogether, these events attract thousands of enterprise technology professionals seeking practical solutions for AI, communications, cloud, cybersecurity, networking, digital transformation, and business automation.  \nSpeakers must adhere 100% to our \"NO COMMERCIALISM\" Policy. If you cannot avoid commercialism, please do not agree to speak at AI Agent Event. Speakers must share their information in an educational, non-commercial and non-self-promotional manner. Speakers must present new concepts and new ideas, not available from any other source. Speakers must provide headshot and complete bio for the conference Web site. Submit your session proposal today and help define the future of Autonomous AI.  \nPlease Provide Speaker Information:  \nPlease Provide Non-Speaker Contact Information: (Contact Person, Speaker Coordinator or Representing Agency)  \nContact Dave Rodriguez at 203-852-6800 x146 or via email for more information on how to expand on these benefits and have an even bigger presence at AI Agent Event 2027.\n\n---\n\n## ICRMV 2027-2027 11th International Conference on Robotics and Machine Vision--iConf\nURL: https://www.iconf.org/conference/icrmv2027\n\nAutomation SciencesRobotartificial intelligence\nLogin Register Toggle navigation Home Conferences News About us Contact us (ICRMV 2027) 2027 11th International Conference on Robotics and Machine Vision Automation Sciences Robot artificial intelligence Conference Date Mar 19-Mar 21, 2027 Place Haining, China Submission Deadline Nov 01, 2026 E-mail icrmv\\_conf@163.com Website https://www.icrmv.org/ Telephone +1-562-606-1057 Description The 11th International Conference on Robotics and Machine Vision (ICRMV 2027) will be held on March 19–21, 2027, in Haining, China. ICRMV aims to provide an interactive forum for learning, collaboration, and networking for researchers in the fields of Robotics and Machine Vision. ★Call for paper★ Topics of interest for submission include, but are not limited to: 1. Machine Learning in Robotics \\*Supervised, unsupervised, and reinforcement learning for robotics \\*Adaptive and autonomous robot control systems \\*Robot learning from demonstration and imitation \\*Learning-based motion planning and navigation \\*Multi-robot learning and coordination 2. Deep Learning in Robotics \\*Deep reinforcement learning for robotic control \\*Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) for robotic perception \\*Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) for sequential robot tasks \\*Deep learning for robot localization and mapping (SLAM) \\*End-to-end learning for robotic manipulation and locomotion 3. Machine Vision and Perception \\*Image and video analysis for robotic applications \\*3D vision, structure from motion, and depth\n\\*Image and video analysis for robotic applications \\*3D vision, structure from motion, and depth perception \\*Visual tracking, object detection, and pose estimation \\*Semantic and instance segmentation for scene understanding \\*Vision-based manipulation, grasping, and tactile-visual integration 4. Robotics Fundamentals and Control \\*Robot kinematics, dynamics, and motion planning \\*Multi-robot systems, swarm robotics, and coordination \\*Human-robot interaction and collaborative robotics \\*Soft robotics, bio-inspired robotics, and reconfigurable robots \\*Robot perception, state estimation, and sensor networks 5. Applications of Machine Learning and Vision in Robotics \\*Healthcare, surgical, and rehabilitation robotics \\*Industrial automation, manufacturing, and logistics \\*Autonomous vehicles, drones, and intelligent transportation systems \\*Service, social, and assistive robotics \\*Agricultural, field, and forestry robotics 6. Theoretical Foundations and Real-World Deployments \\*Advances in neural network architectures for robotic vision \\*Statistical learning, scalability, and real-time efficiency \\*Explainability, interpretability, and safety of robotic learning models \\*Robustness, generalization, and domain adaptation in real-world environments \\*Benchmarking, performance evaluation, and case studies of deployed systems For more topics, please visit at https://icrmv.org/cfp.html ★Publication★ ICRMV 2027 accepted papers will be collected in ICRMV 2027 Conference\n\n---\n\n## International Conference on Computational Vision ICCV in February 2027 in Lisbon\nURL: https://waset.org/computational-vision-conference-in-february-2027-in-lisbon\n\nComputational Vision scheduled on February 04-05, 2027 in February 2027 in Lisbon is for the researchers, scientists, scholars, engineers, academic, scientific and university practitioners to present research activities that might want to attend ...\nComputational Vision scheduled on February 04-05, 2027 in February 2027 in Lisbon is for the researchers, scientists, scholars, engineers, academic, scientific and university practitioners to present research activities that might want to attend events, meetings, seminars, congresses, workshops, summit, and symposiums.\n\n---\n\n## eXCV Workshop at ECCV 2026\nURL: https://excv-workshop.github.io/\n\nWe invite papers that have been previously published at a leading international conference on computer vision or machine learning in 2025 or 2026 (e.g., ECCV, ICCV, CVPR, NeurIPS, ICLR, ICML, AAAI).\nPapers in this track will not be published in the ECCV 2026 Workshop Proceedings. We invite papers that have been previously published at a leading international conference on computer vision or machine learning in 2025 or 2026 (e.g., ECCV, ICCV, CVPR, NeurIPS, ICLR, ICML, AAAI).\neXCV Workshop at ICCV 2025\n\n---\n\n## Call for Papers\nURL: https://iccv.thecvf.com/Conferences/2025/CallForPapers\n\n### Call for Papers  \nPapers in the main technical program must describe high-quality, original research. Topics of interest cover all aspects of computer vision and pattern recognition including, but not limited to:  \nImportant Dates  \n| Paper Registration Deadline\\*: | March 3rd, 2025 11:59pm HST |\n|---|---|\n| Submission and Supplementary Materials Deadline\\*: | March 7th, 2025 11:59pm HST |\n| Reviews Released: | May 9th, 2025 |\n| Rebuttal Period: | May 10-16, 2025 |\n| Final Decisions: | June 26th, 2025 |  \nPaper Registration Deadline\\*: March 3rd, 2025 11:59pm HST Submission and Supplementary Materials Deadline\\*: March 7th, 2025 11:59pm HST Reviews Rele  \n\\* Date is fixed, no extension will be given\n### Paper Submission  \nAll submissions will be handled electronically via the OpenReview conference submission website https://openreview.net/group?id=thecvf.com/ICCV/2025/Conference. All authors must agree to the policies stipulated below. The paper and supplementary materials submission deadline is March 7th, 2025 11:59pm HST and will not be changed.\n### Policies  \nIn submitting a manuscript to ICCV, authors acknowledge that no paper substantially similar in content has been or will be submitted to another conference or workshop during the review period (March 7, 2025 – June 20, 2025). Please refer to the Author Guidelines on the conference website for additional details on dual submissions and guidelines concerning prior work.  \nBy submitting a paper to ICCV, the authors agree to the review process and understand that papers are processed by OpenReview to match each manuscript to the best possible area chairs and reviewers.  \nAll accepted papers will be made publicly available by the Computer Vision Foundation (CVF) two weeks before the conference. Authors wishing to submit a patent understand that the paper's official public disclosure is two weeks before the conference or whenever the authors make it publicly available, whichever is first. More information about CVF is available at http://www.cv-foundation.org/.\n\n---\n\n"],"manifest":{"filesRead":["/Users/sid/.pi/agent/skills/improve/SKILL.md","benchmarks/RESEARCH_RUNBOOK.md","benchmarks/terminal-bench/adapter.ts",".venv_harbor/lib/python3.12/site-packages/harbor/trial/single_step.py",".venv_harbor/lib/python3.12/site-packages/harbor/trial/trial.py","benchmarks/results/terminal-bench/bq-smoke-fix-git-batch-20260721T112914Z/fix-git__iBKNNXz/result.json","benchmarks/results/terminal-bench/pilot-20260721T114236Z/fix-git-batch/fix-git__E8pAbxq/lock.json","benchmarks/terminal-bench/run.sh","/Users/sid/.pi/agent/skills/humanizer/SKILL.md"],"filesModified":["benchmarks/RESEARCH_RUNBOOK.md","benchmarks/terminal_bench